This module covers the structure of IPv4 addresses and how they are used to identify devices on a network. I learned how IPv4 addresses are divided into network and host portions.

Topics Covered
IPv4 address structure
Binary and decimal notation
32-bit IPv4 addresses
Network and host portions
Subnet masks
Network addresses and host addresses
IPv4 address types

What I Learned
An IPv4 address is 32 bits long.
An IPv4 address is divided into four octets.
Each octet contains 8 bits.
IPv4 addresses are usually written in decimal format, such as 192.168.1.10.
A subnet mask identifies the network and host portions of an IP address.
Network addresses identify networks, while host addresses identify devices within a network.
Binary numbers are used to understand how IPv4 addressing works.

Example
IPv4 Address: 192.168.1.10
First Octet: 192
Second Octet: 168
Third Octet: 1
Fourth Octet: 10
Total Length: 32 bits

This module helped me understand IPv4 addressing, binary notation, and the role of subnet masks in identifying network and host portions.

